## The statistical sufficiency check

This is the part I would most want to be asked about, and it is easiest to show working.

After a query executes and **before any answer text is written**, a deterministic check runs over
the result set. It finds the smallest denominator behind a ratio, compares it to a floor declared
in the semantic layer, and runs a two-proportion z-test — or chi-square across more than two
groups — before the answer is allowed to rank anything.

<Shot src="/img/scout-ask.png"
      alt="The Ask panel answering 'Which carrier has the highest delay rate?'. The answer reads: GLS shows the highest delay rate at 25.0%, but that is 2 of 8 — not enough to distinguish it from the 14.9% overall rate. USPS at 23.4% across 47 is the more meaningful signal. Below it a Verified badge and the note: 4 of 9 carrier groups meet the minimum sample of 30."
      title="Asked for the worst carrier, it declines to name the worst carrier"
      caption="GLS tops the raw ranking at 25.0% — on 2 orders. The system says so, and redirects to the carrier whose rate rests on 47. Without this check the honest-looking answer is 'GLS', and someone acts on it." />

The failure this prevents is the normal case, not an edge case. Thin slices are a property of
segmentation itself, so the problem does not go away with more data — it moves to whichever
segment the user drills into next. Putting the test between execution and the answer is the only
place it reliably covers both the chat and the dashboard tiles.

## The model writes a plan, not SQL

The obvious build is to have the model write SQL. It demos well, and it means the model's output
reaches your database, where every failure is a database failure.

<Flow steps={[
  { label: 'Question', detail: '"Which carrier has the highest delay rate?"' },
  { label: 'Planner — one forced tool call', detail: 'The model emits a typed query plan: metrics, dimensions, filters, time range, ordering, limit. It never sees SQL and never writes it.', kind: 'model' },
  { label: 'Validate against the semantic layer', detail: 'Unknown metric, ungroupable dimension, window outside coverage, unbounded scan — each a distinct error code, rejected before execution', kind: 'gate' },
  { label: 'Compile to SQL', detail: 'Code owns this. One portable date function, mapped per dialect' },
  { label: 'Execute' },
  { label: 'Sufficiency check', detail: 'Minimum group size, two-proportion or chi-square test — decides what the answer may claim', kind: 'gate' },
  { label: 'Answer, with its plan attached', detail: 'Every number carries the plan, the resolved window, row counts and the checks that ran' }
]} note="Orange = the model's only decision. Black = a blocking check. The dashboard tiles enter at the validate step and go through the same door as the chat." />

It is also not an agent loop. Routing is one field on the plan, so the model makes one decision
per question rather than reacting across several turns — what varies most between models is how
many decisions they can chain without drifting, so the design removes the chaining.

## One definition of every metric

A single YAML file defines every metric, dimension, grain, valid grouping and time anchor.
Nothing in the worker or the frontend defines what a metric *means*. Changing the meaning of
"delayed" is an edit to that file plus a rebuild, and conformance tests fail if a definition
leaks into code.

<Shot src="/img/scout-explain.png"
      alt="An explain popover over the Total orders tile, showing Interpretation: all orders in scope whatever their status; Metrics: orders is count distinct of order_id, units is sum of quantity; Filters: none; and a footer reading layer v2026.09.08a, 9 ms, cached."
      title="Every number opens to its definition"
      caption="Interpretation, the metric expressions, the filters in force, and which version of the semantic layer produced it. A dashboard and a chat that can disagree about what a number means are two products." />

## Forecasting that is willing to refuse

Three methods — moving average, linear trend, simple exponential smoothing — selected by backtest
error, with prediction intervals from the residual spread, and an inventory recommendation derived
from demand variance over the lead time at a chosen service level.

<Shot src="/img/scout-forecast.png"
      alt="Forecast view: a projected-units chart with history through December 2025 and a dashed forecast with a widening interval band to April 2026, labelled 'moving average, chosen by backtest'. A warning reads: only 12 observations, every series on this dataset falls below the 24-observation threshold, so treat the direction as indicative and the interval as the real answer. A method panel lists moving average MAPE 0.115 selected, ses 0.160 and linear trend 0.439 as losing candidates. An inventory panel shows demand over lead time 66.5, safety stock at z 1.64 of 19.2, reorder point 85.7 and order-up-to level 180.7."
      title="The method panel shows what lost, and the banner says not to trust the line"
      caption="Moving average won at MAPE 0.115 against SES 0.160 and linear trend 0.439. With 12 monthly points every series trips the low-confidence threshold, and the caption under the chart reads 'the band is the answer here, not the line'. A SKU with too little history is refused outright, with a fallback pointing at an aggregation level that has enough data." />

The guards matter more than the projection. Fitting a trend through a handful of sparse points
produces a number whose interval is wide enough to be meaningless, and putting it next to a chart
makes it look considered. The model's only role here is extracting parameters from the question;
every number is computed.

## Engineering notes

- **Tests** across the compiler, validator, time resolution, forecast selection and guards,
  sufficiency statistics, golden-output snapshots, and a dialect parity suite that checks the
  SQLite and Postgres emitters agree.
- **Portable SQL by construction** — metric definitions use plain column references and one
  portable date function the compiler maps per dialect. The parity tests keep that true.
- **Secrets never reach the client.** The browser bundle carries no metric definitions,
  thresholds or glossary; those stay server-side. The auth gate fails closed, because the
  opposite default turns one forgotten deployment step into a public dataset.
- **Degrades honestly.** Dashboard, forecasts and every explain panel work with no model access
  at all — they are hand-written plans. Only the chat needs a key, and it says so rather than
  failing opaquely.

## Stack

TypeScript throughout. React and Recharts on the client, Cloudflare Workers and D1 on the server,
Zod for schema validation, Vitest for tests, deployed with Wrangler.
